    <statement><p>Suppose that
<me>f(x+h)-f(x) = -2 h x^2  +    1 h x  -    5 h^2 x  +    2 h^2  +    5 h^3.</me>
Find <m>f'(x)</m>.</p>
<p><m>f'(x) =</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-2x^{2}+1x</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The definition of the derivative is
<me>f'(x)= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}</me>
This problem gives us the numerator, <m>f(x+h)-f(x)</m>.  So,</p>
<p><me>f'(x)= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{-2 h x^2  +    1 h x  -    5 h^2 x  +    2 h^2  +    5 h^3}{h} = \lim_{h\to 0} -2 x^2  +    1 x  -    5 h x  +    2 h  +    5 h^2 = -2 x^2+1 x</me></p>
</solution>
